  "trace_id": "hybrid::q03",
  "architecture": "hybrid",
  "question": "What is the difference between recall@k and MRR as retrieval metrics?",
  "answer": "Recall\u202f@\u202fk and mean reciprocal rank (MRR) measure different aspects of a retriever\u2019s output:\n\n* **Recall\u202f@\u202fk** asks, \u201cIs the correct supporting chunk present anywhere among the top\u202fk retrieved chunks?\u201d\u202fIt is a binary yes/no per query that is then averaged over the dataset. Because it ignores the exact position of the correct chunk, a hit at rank\u202f1 and a hit at rank\u202fk receive the same score, making it useful for checking whether a downstream step even has a chance to see the right evidence\u202f[retrieval-metrics-recall-at-k-and-mrr::0].\n\n* **MRR** looks at the rank of the *first* correct chunk: each query is scored\u202f`1 / rank`, so a hit at rank\u202f1 gets\u202f1.0, at rank\u202f2 gets\u202f0.5, at rank\u202f10 gets\u202f0.1, etc. The scores are then averaged. This metric penalizes a retriever that finds the right chunk but buries it low in the list, so it is more sensitive to ordering quality\u202f[retrieval-metrics-recall-at-k-and-mrr::0].",
